WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.480
What would you do if you could assemble your
very own Avengers Initiative?

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.560
Better yet, what would Jason Mewes do?

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.980
Best known as Jay of Jay and Silent Bob fame,
Mewes sat down with us while promoting his

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.580
new movie, Madness in the Method, which he
both stars in and directed.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.539
Now that Mewes is a full-fledged filmmaker
who's been in charge of his own production,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:22.560
we decided to step things up a notch and ask
him what he'd do with more magnificent responsibilities.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.380
To be specific, we asked the lifelong comic
book fan what he would do if he were in the

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.080
Nick Fury role and had the opportunity to
recruit his own team of Avengers from Marvel

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.320
Comics' vast pool of characters.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.520
And we're not just talking about good guys,
either.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:38.400
Villains switch sides in comics all the time,
sometimes for weird reasons.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.390
Why wouldn't they do the same for a foul-mouthed
stoner from New Jersey who's bold enough to

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.760
wield a bong-saber and brave enough to unload
a machine gun at a nigh-unkillable angel?

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.840
For one, Mewes has been a comic book fan all
his life, an affinity that's been reflected

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.300
in Kevin Smith's movies and the characters
they play in them.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.640
The drug-addled duo has appeared in both Smith's
movies and real-life comic books as their

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.540
superhero alter egos Bluntman and Chronic.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.840
They've also met and worked with Marvel Comics
icon Stan Lee on multiple occasions — with

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.000
Lee having a cameo in Smith's Mallrats, and
another cameo, decades later, in Mewes' Madness

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.660
in the Method.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.000
These aren't just hat-tips to the nerd crowd;
Mewes has an enthusiasm for comic books that's

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.760
downright infectious, and as an actor, he's
even made appearances in the CW's Arrowverse.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.200
"Get some free comics, read the comics, love
and sniff the comics."

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.880
So now that you know his bona fides, the question
remains: What would Jason Mewes' team of Avengers

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:29.460
look like?

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:34.040
Though the Avengers' roster has grown post-Endgame
to include what feels like hundreds of people

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:37.940
from all around the universe — including
sorcerers, Asgardians, and even Howard the

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.890
Duck — the first Avengers movie showed the
promise of the shared Marvel universe with

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.980
just six heroes.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.540
So, we'll go with six.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.880
Like many would, Mewes started off his selection
with three major powerhouses: the incredible

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.420
Hulk, the adamantium-infused mutant Wolverine,
and the immensely-overpowered Spider-Man.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.380
But after that, things got interesting.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.020
"It could be villains too, right?"

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.460
Mewes indeed went for the villains, but didn't
waste time with the lower-level likes of Crossbones,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.120
Bullseye, or some New York City bank robbers.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.460
He went straight for the god-tier.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.020
"So should we say Thanos?

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.200
Apocalypse?"

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.400
With three slots to go, Mewes picked both,
instantly making his team one of the most

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.440
formidable forces of destruction in the entire
universe.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.680
Not very heroic, maybe, but hey, they'll get
the job done — at least, so long as there's

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.400
someone a little more level-headed in charge.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.720
It takes more than pure power to make a good
team.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.000
If only to give great motivational speeches,
every would-be band of superheroes needs a

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.150
leader — and no, neither Bluntman nor Chronic
are up for the picking.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.419
"You need a team leader, right?

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.980
I feel like you almost need Cap.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.379
Iron Man or Captain America.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.280
[...] Or, you know what?

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.660
Cyclops."

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.560
Poaching the frequent leader of the X-Men
is a bold move, and it inspired Mewes to make

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.510
some other changes to his roster before locking
it in.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:57.140
"I love Spider-Man, but I feel like I was
just thinking about Magneto.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.380
I mean the powerhouses.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.560
I feel like [my team's] too powerful, but
I think it would be pretty crazy, and great."

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.800
We'll hand it to him — Magneto subbing in
for Spider-Man to stand alongside Apocalypse,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:14.400
Wolverine, and Cyclops is pretty great, even
if it's not as crazy as imagining Spider-Man

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.600
and Thanos teaming up — especially after
what happened in the movies.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.100
Mewes ended up sending Spider-Man to the curb,
instead settling on Cyclops as a leader of

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:22.660
the team.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.140
With that, he locked in his own personal squad
of New Jersey Avengers.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.160
We already have the Hulk.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:36.520
And then we have Thanos, Apocalypse, Magneto,
Cyclops is the leader, and Wolverine.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.100
So that's six I believe [...] unless I said
Wolverine twice again.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.300
With a team half made up of heroes and half
of slightly-less-heroic figures, Jason Mewes

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.300
assembled a strange configuration of Avengers,
heavy on the X-Men and the heavy-hitters.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.640
It's the kind of squad that makes you wonder
how the team would really work together.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:54.180
Thanos, Apocalypse, or Magneto turning face
to play nice with the good guys is something

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.519
that makes sense… on an individual basis.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:00.519
But imagining all three of them working together
for a common cause without infighting?

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.680
That's a little harder to figure.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.809
We don't care how cool it is — Mewes' team
wouldn't last six issues before they all tried

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:06.809
to kill each other.

00:04:06.809 --> 00:04:10.799
Despite that potential problem, Mewes shared
only one regret about the constitution of

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:14.709
his superhero squad: the fact that he didn't
have any room on there for someone who runs

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:15.700
really, really fast.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.300
"I should have had a Speedster.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:18.620
Again, it’s tough.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.700
Quicksilver would be awesome.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:21.760
But I'm going to stick with that.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.080
So there's my team."

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.940
On top of checking out his directorial debut
Madness in the Method, you can catch Jason

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.870
Mewes in the upcoming film Jay and Silent
Bob Reboot, touring the United States in a

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.360
roadshow format starting October 15.

